    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d300">

                <pb facs="0441" n="331" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg331"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/2316: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-27T17:00:00-05:00">October 27, 1931—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>397. Your 818, October 24, 11 a.m.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d300-f1"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>You may instruct Mukden to inform the radio corporation’s representative
                        at Shanghai that the question of the Mukden wireless station is receiving
                        the Department’s consideration.</item>
                    <pb facs="0442" n="332" xml:id="pg332"/>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The Embassy at Tokyo reported on October 20<anchor n="26" xml:id="d300-f2"
                        /> that it had been informed orally by the Foreign Office that this station
                        would soon be opened with South Manchuria Railway operators. Japanese
                        Ambassador here told press that it would be restored to its owners. Instruct
                        Mukden to report through you the present situation in regard to this
                        station.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
